# 13.3 Flows with Variable Fluid Properties

## Variable fluid properties

Main fluid properties:

$$
\rho = \text{density}
$$

$$
\mu = \text{dynamic viscosity}
$$

$$
\nu = \frac{\mu}{\rho} = \text{kinematic viscosity}
$$

$$
k = \text{thermal conductivity}
$$

$$
c_p = \text{specific heat capacity}
$$

For a constant-property fluid:

$$
\rho,\mu,k,c_p = \text{constant}
$$

For a variable-property fluid, one or more properties depend on other variables, especially temperature and pressure:

$$
\rho = \rho(T,p)
$$

$$
\mu = \mu(T)
$$

$$
k = k(T)
$$

Therefore, changes in temperature or pressure can modify the properties of the fluid and consequently the flow itself.

---

## Coupling between properties and the flow

Example with temperature and density:

$$
T \rightarrow \rho \rightarrow \mathbf{u}
$$

but the velocity field also transports temperature:

$$
\mathbf{u} \rightarrow T
$$

Therefore:

$$
T \leftrightarrow \rho \leftrightarrow \mathbf{u}
$$

The variables cannot always be calculated independently.

A typical numerical sequence is:

$$
T^{(n)}
\rightarrow
\rho^{(n)}
\rightarrow
\mathbf{u}^{(n)}
\rightarrow
T^{(n+1)}
\rightarrow \dots
$$

The fluid properties are updated when a new solution for the variables becomes available.

---

## Outer iterations

When the properties depend on the solution, one solution of each equation is usually not sufficient.

During one iteration, the currently available values of the properties are used.

After solving the equations:

$$
\text{solve flow}
\rightarrow
\text{update properties}
\rightarrow
\text{solve again}
$$

This repeated cycle is an **outer iteration**.

Outer iterations are also needed because of other nonlinear or coupled quantities, for example convection terms and turbulence-model variables.

---

## Under-relaxation

Strong property variations can make the numerical system difficult to converge.

Instead of immediately accepting the complete new value of a variable:

$$
\phi_{\text{new}}
$$

only part of the change can be applied:

$$
\phi^{(n)}
=
\phi^{(n-1)}
+
\alpha
\left(
\phi_{\text{new}}-\phi^{(n-1)}
\right)
$$

where:

$$
0 < \alpha \leq 1
$$

$\alpha$ = under-relaxation factor.

Smaller $\alpha$:

$$
\text{smaller change per iteration}
\rightarrow
\text{greater stability}
$$

but also:

$$
\text{more iterations}
$$

Strongly coupled or **stiff** problems can require stronger under-relaxation and more outer iterations.

---

## Steady and transient problems

### Steady flow

No physical time evolution is required.

The iterations continue until:

$$
\text{solution}^{(n+1)}
\approx
\text{solution}^{(n)}
$$

Multigrid methods can be used to accelerate convergence.

### Transient flow

The solution advances through physical time:

$$
t^n \rightarrow t^{n+1}
$$

If the time step $\Delta t$ is small, the solution at the previous time step is already a good estimate for the next one.

Outer iterations can still be required inside each time step to update:

$$
\text{fluid properties}
+
\text{nonlinear terms}
+
\text{turbulence quantities}
$$

---

## Connection to atmospheric flows

Air properties can vary because of temperature.

For density:

$$
T \uparrow
\Rightarrow
\rho \downarrow
$$

$$
T \downarrow
\Rightarrow
\rho \uparrow
$$

When gravity is present:

$$
\text{density difference}
+
g
\rightarrow
\text{buoyancy}
$$

Warm, less-dense air tends to rise.

Cold, denser air tends to sink.

Therefore:

$$
T
\rightarrow
\rho
\rightarrow
\text{buoyancy}
\rightarrow
\text{vertical motion}
$$

This relation is important in atmospheric boundary-layer simulations.

---

## Boussinesq approximation

Useful when density variations are small:

$$
\Delta \rho \ll \rho_0
$$

Density is treated as approximately constant in most terms:

$$
\rho \approx \rho_0
$$

but its variation is retained where it produces buoyancy.

A common approximation is:

$$
\rho
=
\rho_0
\left[
1-\beta(T-T_0)
\right]
$$

where:

$\rho_0$ = reference density

$T_0$ = reference temperature

$\beta$ = thermal expansion coefficient

Main idea:

$$
\boxed{
\text{small density variations}
+
\text{gravity}
\rightarrow
\text{important buoyancy effects}
}
$$

---

# 13.5 Free-Surface Flows

## Free surface

A **free surface** is the interface between two fluids, typically a liquid and a gas.

Example:

$$
\text{air}
$$

$$
-------------------- \text{ free surface}
$$

$$
\text{water}
$$

The main numerical problem is that the position of the interface is not known in advance.

It changes with time:

$$
\Gamma = \Gamma(t)
$$

Therefore, the simulation must determine:

$$
\text{fluid motion}
+
\text{interface position}
$$

Typical applications:

water waves, ship flows, open channels, sloshing, breaking waves.

---

## Conditions at the free surface

### Kinematic condition

The interface moves with the fluid.

Conceptually:

$$
\text{fluid velocity}
\rightarrow
\text{interface motion}
$$

The interface should not move independently of the fluid that forms it.

### Dynamic condition

Forces acting on the two sides of the interface must be balanced.

Relevant effects:

$$
\text{pressure}
+
\text{viscous stress}
+
\text{surface tension}
$$

For a simple water-air interface, atmospheric pressure often provides the pressure condition on the gas side.

---

# 13.5.1 Interface-Capturing Methods

## General idea

The computational mesh remains fixed.

$$
\boxed{\text{fixed mesh}}
$$

The interface is **not represented directly by mesh points**.

Instead, an additional variable indicates which fluid is present in each control volume.

The interface is therefore reconstructed from the solution.

---

## Volume of Fluid – VOF

VOF = **Volume of Fluid method**.

A volume fraction is introduced, usually written as $\alpha$.

For an air-water example:

$$
\alpha = 1
\Rightarrow
\text{cell completely filled with water}
$$

$$
\alpha = 0
\Rightarrow
\text{cell completely filled with air}
$$

$$
0 < \alpha < 1
\Rightarrow
\text{interface crosses the cell}
$$

Example:

| Cell | $\alpha$ | Meaning |
|---|---:|---|
| 1 | 0 | air |
| 2 | 0 | air |
| 3 | 0.25 | interface |
| 4 | 0.80 | interface |
| 5 | 1 | water |
| 6 | 1 | water |

The interface is approximately located in the cells where:

$$
0<\alpha<1
$$

A commonly used reference location is around:

$$
\alpha = 0.5
$$

---

## Transport of the volume fraction

The volume fraction moves with the fluid.

For a simple incompressible case:

$$
\frac{\partial \alpha}{\partial t}
+
\mathbf{u}\cdot\nabla\alpha
=
0
$$

Meaning:

$$
\mathbf{u}
\rightarrow
\text{transport of }\alpha
\rightarrow
\text{movement of interface}
$$

If the water moves to another part of the domain, the distribution of $\alpha$ must move with it.

---

## Fluid properties in VOF

The local properties depend on the volume fraction.

For density:

$$
\rho
=
\alpha\rho_1
+
(1-\alpha)\rho_2
$$

For viscosity:

$$
\mu
=
\alpha\mu_1
+
(1-\alpha)\mu_2
$$

Therefore:

$$
\alpha
\rightarrow
\rho,\mu
\rightarrow
\text{momentum equation}
$$

One velocity field is solved, while the local properties change depending on which phase occupies the cell.

---

## Main numerical problem: interface smearing

Real interface:

$$
0 \quad 0 \quad 0 \quad | \quad 1 \quad 1 \quad 1
$$

Possible numerical result:

$$
0 \quad 0.15 \quad 0.35 \quad 0.60 \quad 0.85 \quad 1
$$

The transition becomes spread over several cells.

This is **numerical diffusion** or interface smearing.

Goal:

$$
\text{transport }\alpha
$$

while maintaining:

$$
0\leq\alpha\leq1
$$

and keeping:

$$
\text{interface as sharp as possible}
$$

A very diffused interface gives a less accurate position of the free surface.

---

## Advantages of interface capturing

The mesh does not need to follow the interface.

Therefore, large deformations are easier to handle:

$$
\text{waves}
\rightarrow
\text{breaking}
\rightarrow
\text{droplets}
\rightarrow
\text{merging/splitting}
$$

The method is especially useful when the topology of the interface changes.

---

# 13.5.2 Interface-Tracking Methods

## General idea

The interface is represented explicitly.

The mesh points located at the free surface move with it.

$$
\boxed{\text{interface explicitly tracked}}
$$

For example, the surface can be written as:

$$
z=H(x,y,t)
$$

where $H$ is the local free-surface height.

---

## Mesh movement

When the free surface moves:

$$
\text{surface motion}
\rightarrow
\text{surface mesh motion}
\rightarrow
\text{interior mesh adaptation}
$$

The position of the interface is therefore accurately known.

Main advantage:

$$
\text{sharp and accurate interface}
$$

Main difficulty:

$$
\text{large deformation}
\rightarrow
\text{mesh distortion}
$$

For a simple smooth surface, adapting the mesh can be manageable.

For:

$$
\text{breaking waves}
+
\text{droplets}
+
\text{merging}
+
\text{splitting}
$$

interface tracking becomes much more difficult.

---

## Interface capturing vs interface tracking

| Interface capturing | Interface tracking |
|---|---|
| Fixed mesh | Moving/deforming mesh |
| Interface found from a phase variable | Interface explicitly represented |
| Easier for large deformation | Accurate surface location |
| Can handle topology changes | Difficult when topology changes |
| Example: VOF | Example: moving-grid method |

---

# 13.5.3 Hybrid Methods

Hybrid methods combine characteristics of both approaches:

$$
\text{interface capturing}
+
\text{interface tracking}
$$

The objective is:

$$
\text{robustness of a fixed grid}
+
\text{accurate interface location}
$$

One possibility is to solve the flow on a fixed mesh while using marker particles to explicitly follow the interface.

The markers move according to the velocity field:

$$
\mathbf{u}
\rightarrow
\text{marker motion}
\rightarrow
\text{interface position}
$$

At the same time, the fluid properties can be treated as variable across the interface.

---

## VOF-PLIC

PLIC = **Piecewise Linear Interface Construction**.

The volume fraction $\alpha$ is still calculated as in VOF.

However, instead of only knowing that the interface passes through a cell, the interface is geometrically reconstructed.

In 2D:

$$
\text{interface}
\approx
\text{line segment}
$$

In 3D:

$$
\text{interface}
\approx
\text{plane}
$$

The normal direction is estimated from the volume-fraction gradient:

$$
\mathbf{n}
\propto
\nabla\alpha
$$

Therefore:

$$
\alpha
\rightarrow
\text{interface orientation}
\rightarrow
\text{geometric reconstruction}
$$

This reduces interface smearing and gives a more accurate interface shape.

---

## Froude number

Free-surface flows are strongly influenced by gravity.

The Froude number compares inertia with gravitational effects:

$$
Fr
=
\frac{U}{\sqrt{gL}}
$$

For open-channel flows, the characteristic length can be the water depth $h$:

$$
Fr
=
\frac{U}{\sqrt{gh}}
$$

### Subcritical flow

$$
Fr<1
$$

Gravity-wave propagation is fast compared with the fluid velocity.

Disturbances can propagate upstream.

### Critical flow

$$
Fr=1
$$

Fluid velocity and gravity-wave speed are comparable.

### Supercritical flow

$$
Fr>1
$$

Flow velocity is larger than the gravity-wave speed.

Disturbances cannot propagate upstream.

---

# 13.7 Meteorological and Oceanographic Applications

## Characteristic scales

Atmospheric and oceanographic flows occur over extremely large distances.

Typical relation:

$$
L_{\text{horizontal}}
\gg
L_{\text{vertical}}
$$

Possible horizontal scale:

$$
10^3 \text{ km}
$$

while the vertical scale can be only a few kilometres.

Therefore, these flows have a very large **aspect ratio**.

Large-scale motion can behave approximately as a two-dimensional flow, while smaller-scale turbulence remains fully three-dimensional.

Vertical motion can still be physically important.

---

## Reynolds number

$$
Re
=
\frac{UL}{\nu}
$$

Atmospheric and oceanographic flows have very large characteristic lengths $L$.

Therefore:

$$
Re\gg1
$$

and:

$$
\boxed{\text{turbulence is fundamental}}
$$

Viscosity is important mainly at the smallest turbulent scales, while inertia dominates the large-scale flow.

---

## Different scales → different dominant physics

The same equations do not imply that every physical term is equally important at every scale.

Large scales:

$$
\text{Earth rotation important}
$$

Smaller scales:

$$
\text{3D turbulence and stratification important}
$$

Therefore:

$$
\text{scale}
\rightarrow
\text{dominant physical effects}
\rightarrow
\text{numerical model}
$$

Phenomena that are smaller than the computational grid cannot be resolved directly.

They must be:

$$
\boxed{\text{modelled}}
$$

This is especially important in meteorology because the physical scales range from very small turbulent eddies to continental and global circulation.

---

## Earth rotation and Coriolis force

For sufficiently large spatial and temporal scales, Earth's rotation affects the motion.

In a rotating reference frame:

$$
\mathbf{a}_C
=
-2\boldsymbol{\Omega}
\times
\mathbf{u}
$$

where:

$\boldsymbol{\Omega}$ = angular velocity of Earth

$\mathbf{u}$ = fluid velocity

$\mathbf{a}_C$ = Coriolis acceleration

Large-scale atmospheric flow:

$$
\text{Coriolis effect}
\rightarrow
\text{important}
$$

Very small-scale flow:

$$
\text{Coriolis effect}
\rightarrow
\text{often negligible}
$$

In the Northern Hemisphere, moving air is deflected to the right relative to its direction of motion.

In the Southern Hemisphere, it is deflected to the left.

---

## Stratification

Stratification = systematic variation of density in the vertical direction.

$$
\rho=\rho(z)
$$

In the atmosphere, density stratification is strongly connected to temperature.

In the ocean, it can depend on:

$$
\text{temperature}
+
\text{salinity}
$$

Stable stratification tends to oppose vertical displacement.

Therefore:

$$
\text{strong stable stratification}
\rightarrow
\text{vertical mixing reduced}
$$

---

## Hydrostatic approximation

Vertical momentum equation contains pressure, gravity and vertical acceleration.

If vertical acceleration is small:

$$
\frac{Dw}{Dt}\approx0
$$

then pressure and gravity approximately balance:

$$
\frac{\partial p}{\partial z}
=
-\rho g
$$

This is the **hydrostatic approximation**.

Meaning:

$$
\text{vertical pressure gradient}
\approx
\text{gravity}
$$

Useful for large-scale atmospheric and ocean models where vertical accelerations are relatively small.

---

## Nonhydrostatic models

The hydrostatic approximation becomes less appropriate when vertical acceleration is important.

Examples:

$$
\text{turbulent eddies}
$$

$$
\text{convection}
$$

$$
\text{cloud dynamics}
$$

$$
\text{high-resolution ABL flow}
$$

A nonhydrostatic model retains vertical acceleration.

Therefore:

$$
\boxed{
\text{high resolution / turbulence}
\rightarrow
\text{nonhydrostatic formulation often needed}
}
$$

---

## Atmospheric Boundary Layer – ABL

The Atmospheric Boundary Layer is the lowest part of the atmosphere directly influenced by the Earth's surface.

Main surface effects:

$$
\text{friction}
+
\text{roughness}
+
\text{heating/cooling}
$$

Near the surface, friction reduces the wind velocity.

With increasing height:

$$
z\uparrow
\Rightarrow
U\uparrow
$$

The vertical change in horizontal velocity is **wind shear**:

$$
\frac{\partial U}{\partial z}
$$

Wind shear produces turbulence:

$$
\text{surface friction}
\rightarrow
\text{wind shear}
\rightarrow
\text{turbulence}
$$

---

## Atmospheric stability

ABL turbulence can also be influenced by buoyancy.

Three main conditions:

$$
\boxed{
\text{stable}
\quad
\text{neutral}
\quad
\text{unstable}
}
$$

### Neutral condition

Buoyancy has little influence:

$$
\text{buoyancy production}
\approx0
$$

Turbulence mainly generated by:

$$
\text{wind shear}
$$

Therefore:

$$
\text{neutral ABL}
\rightarrow
\text{mechanically generated turbulence}
$$

---

### Unstable condition

Surface warmer than air above:

$$
T_{\text{surface}}
>
T_{\text{air}}
$$

Warm air:

$$
T\uparrow
\Rightarrow
\rho\downarrow
\Rightarrow
\text{air rises}
$$

Therefore:

$$
\text{buoyancy}
\rightarrow
\text{vertical mixing}\uparrow
\rightarrow
\text{turbulence}\uparrow
$$

Typical situation: heated surface during daytime.

---

### Stable condition

Surface colder than air above:

$$
T_{\text{surface}}
<
T_{\text{air}}
$$

Cold dense air remains close to the ground.

Vertical displacement is opposed by buoyancy:

$$
\text{vertical mixing}\downarrow
$$

$$
\text{turbulence}\downarrow
$$

Typical situation: nighttime surface cooling.

---

## Potential temperature

Atmospheric pressure decreases with height.

An air parcel that rises expands and cools even without exchanging heat with the environment.

Potential temperature $\theta$ removes this pressure-related temperature change.

$$
\theta
=
T
\left(
\frac{p_0}{p}
\right)^{R_d/c_p}
$$

where:

$T$ = actual temperature

$p$ = local pressure

$p_0$ = reference pressure

$R_d$ = gas constant for dry air

$c_p$ = specific heat at constant pressure

Potential temperature is useful for determining atmospheric stability.

For a dry atmosphere, approximately:

$$
\frac{d\theta}{dz}>0
\Rightarrow
\text{stable}
$$

$$
\frac{d\theta}{dz}\approx0
\Rightarrow
\text{neutral}
$$

$$
\frac{d\theta}{dz}<0
\Rightarrow
\text{unstable}
$$

---

## Turbulence treatment: RANS vs LES

The turbulence model depends on the scale and required resolution.

### RANS

RANS = Reynolds-Averaged Navier-Stokes.

The turbulent fluctuations are not directly resolved.

Their average effect is modelled.

$$
\text{mean flow}
+
\text{turbulence model}
$$

Useful when:

$$
\text{large domain}
+
\text{limited resolution}
$$

---

### LES

LES = Large Eddy Simulation.

Large turbulent structures are resolved directly.

Small turbulent structures are modelled.

$$
\text{large eddies}
\rightarrow
\text{resolved}
$$

$$
\text{small eddies}
\rightarrow
\text{subgrid-scale model}
$$

LES is particularly useful when three-dimensional turbulent structures are important, for example in high-resolution ABL simulations.

---

## Energy cascade

Turbulent energy moves from large structures towards smaller scales:

$$
\text{large eddies}
\rightarrow
\text{medium eddies}
\rightarrow
\text{small eddies}
\rightarrow
\text{viscous dissipation}
$$

LES resolves the first part of this cascade and models the unresolved final part.

---

## Scale and turbulence model

Very schematically:

$$
\text{high-resolution 3D flow}
\rightarrow
\text{LES}
$$

$$
\text{large-scale / averaged flow}
\rightarrow
\text{RANS}
$$

The available computational resources determine the grid resolution.

Therefore:

$$
\text{grid resolution}
\rightarrow
\text{resolved scales}
\rightarrow
\text{required turbulence model}
$$

---

## Time accuracy

Atmospheric and oceanographic flows contain many wave phenomena.

A numerical time scheme should not artificially:

$$
\text{amplify waves}
$$

or:

$$
\text{damp waves}
$$

The book discusses the historical use of the Leapfrog method and the widespread use of Runge-Kutta methods.

A common choice is third-order Runge-Kutta:

$$
\boxed{\text{RK3}}
$$

especially in high-resolution atmospheric simulations.

---

## Main ABL connection

For an atmospheric boundary-layer simulation:

$$
\text{surface roughness}
\rightarrow
\text{wind shear}
\rightarrow
\text{turbulence}
$$

and, when thermal effects are present:

$$
T
\rightarrow
\rho
\rightarrow
\text{buoyancy}
\rightarrow
\text{stability}
\rightarrow
\text{turbulence}
$$

For sufficiently large domains:

$$
\text{Earth rotation}
\rightarrow
\text{Coriolis force}
$$

Therefore, the ABL flow can depend on the interaction of:

$$
\boxed{
\text{pressure gradient}
+
\text{surface friction}
+
\text{Coriolis}
+
\text{turbulence}
+
\text{buoyancy}
}
$$

---

# 13.8 Multiphase Flows

## Definition

A multiphase flow contains more than one physical phase.

Typical combinations:

$$
\text{gas + liquid}
$$

$$
\text{liquid + solid}
$$

$$
\text{gas + solid}
$$

Examples:

solid particles transported by gas → dusty gas

solid particles transported by liquid → slurry

gas bubbles in liquid → bubbly flow

liquid droplets in gas → spray

gas and liquid with a large interface → free-surface flow

---

## Phase change

Multiphase flows can also involve transformation from one phase to another.

Examples:

$$
\text{liquid}
\rightarrow
\text{vapour}
\quad
\text{boiling / cavitation}
$$

$$
\text{vapour}
\rightarrow
\text{liquid}
\quad
\text{condensation}
$$

$$
\text{solid}
\leftrightarrow
\text{liquid}
\quad
\text{melting / solidification}
$$

Phase change introduces transfer of:

$$
\text{mass}
+
\text{momentum}
+
\text{energy}
$$

between phases.

---

## Continuous and dispersed phases

Many multiphase flows can be divided into:

### Continuous or carrier phase

The fluid occupying most of the domain.

Example:

$$
\text{air surrounding droplets}
$$

### Dispersed phase

Particles, droplets or bubbles distributed inside the carrier phase.

Example:

$$
\text{water droplets in air}
$$

The continuous phase is normally solved using an **Eulerian** description.

The dispersed phase can be treated using either:

$$
\text{Lagrangian approach}
$$

or:

$$
\text{Eulerian approach}
$$

---

# Eulerian description

The flow is observed at fixed positions in space.

The computational mesh remains fixed and variables are calculated in each cell:

$$
\mathbf{u}(\mathbf{x},t)
$$

$$
p(\mathbf{x},t)
$$

$$
T(\mathbf{x},t)
$$

Typical CFD finite-volume calculations use this viewpoint for the continuous fluid.

---

# Lagrangian description

Individual particles, droplets or bubbles are followed through the domain.

For one particle:

$$
\mathbf{x}_p(t)
$$

represents its position.

The trajectory follows:

$$
\frac{d\mathbf{x}_p}{dt}
=
\mathbf{u}_p
$$

The forces acting on the particle determine its velocity.

Concept:

$$
\text{fluid field}
\rightarrow
\text{force on particle}
\rightarrow
\text{particle trajectory}
$$

---

## Computational particles or packets

A real spray or dusty flow can contain millions or billions of particles.

Tracking every physical particle is usually impossible.

Therefore:

$$
1\ \text{computational particle}
\rightarrow
N\ \text{real particles}
$$

The computational particle represents a **packet** of particles with similar properties.

This strongly reduces computational cost.

---

## One-way coupling

For a very small dispersed-phase loading:

$$
\text{fluid}
\rightarrow
\text{particles}
$$

but:

$$
\text{particles}
\nrightarrow
\text{fluid significantly}
$$

Procedure:

$$
\text{calculate carrier flow}
\rightarrow
\text{calculate particle trajectories}
$$

The particle feels the fluid, but the fluid does not significantly feel the particle.

This is called **one-way coupling**.

---

## Two-way coupling

If the dispersed phase has a significant mass loading:

$$
\text{fluid}
\leftrightarrow
\text{particles}
$$

The fluid affects the particles.

The particles also transfer quantities back to the fluid:

$$
\text{momentum}
$$

$$
\text{mass}
$$

$$
\text{energy}
$$

The two systems must then be solved together.

This is **two-way coupling**.

Additional models can be required for:

$$
\text{particle collisions}
$$

$$
\text{agglomeration}
$$

$$
\text{particle breakup}
$$

$$
\text{particle-wall interaction}
$$

---

# Eulerian–Eulerian / Two-Fluid Model

For large concentrations of both phases, both phases can be treated as continuous fields.

Phase 1:

$$
\mathbf{u}_1,\quad T_1,\quad \alpha_1
$$

Phase 2:

$$
\mathbf{u}_2,\quad T_2,\quad \alpha_2
$$

For two phases:

$$
\alpha_1+\alpha_2=1
$$

Each phase has its own governing equations.

The phases interact through exchange terms:

$$
\text{momentum exchange}
$$

$$
\text{mass exchange}
$$

$$
\text{energy exchange}
$$

Therefore:

$$
\text{more phases}
\rightarrow
\text{more equations}
\rightarrow
\text{stronger coupling}
$$

The system can become numerically stiff.

Typical consequence:

$$
\text{smaller time steps}
+
\text{stronger under-relaxation}
$$

---

# Homogeneous multiphase model

A simpler approximation assumes **no slip between the phases**.

No slip means:

$$
\mathbf{u}_1=\mathbf{u}_2
$$

The phases also share:

$$
p_1=p_2
$$

and often:

$$
T_1=T_2
$$

Therefore, instead of solving completely separate flow fields:

$$
\text{multiphase mixture}
\rightarrow
\text{one effective fluid}
$$

with variable effective properties.

This greatly reduces the number of equations.

---

# Relation between free-surface and multiphase methods

Free-surface flows are a particular type of multiphase flow.

For a water-air system:

$$
\text{water phase}
+
\text{air phase}
$$

When the interface is relatively clear and the interfacial area is not too large, the methods from Section 13.5 can be used:

$$
\text{VOF}
$$

$$
\text{interface tracking}
$$

$$
\text{hybrid methods}
$$

If there are enormous numbers of small droplets, bubbles or particles, explicitly resolving every interface becomes too computationally expensive.

Then:

$$
\text{Euler-Lagrange}
$$

or:

$$
\text{Euler-Euler}
$$

models become more practical.

---

# Cavitation

Cavitation is a liquid-vapour multiphase phenomenon.

It occurs when the local liquid pressure becomes sufficiently low.

If:

$$
p < p_s
$$

where $p_s$ is the saturation vapour pressure, liquid can vaporize and vapour bubbles can grow.

Therefore:

$$
p\downarrow
\rightarrow
p<p_s
\rightarrow
\text{vapour formation}
$$

When the pressure increases again:

$$
p>p_s
\rightarrow
\text{bubble collapse}
$$

Cavitation is important in:

$$
\text{propellers}
$$

$$
\text{pumps}
$$

$$
\text{turbines}
$$

$$
\text{injectors}
$$

---

## Vapour volume fraction

A homogeneous cavitation model can use the vapour volume fraction:

$$
c_v
$$

Liquid volume fraction:

$$
c_l
$$

For two phases:

$$
c_l+c_v=1
$$

The vapour fraction is transported with the flow, but unlike a normal free-surface VOF equation it also contains a phase-change source term:

$$
\frac{\partial c_v}{\partial t}
+
\nabla\cdot(c_v\mathbf{u})
=
q_v
$$

where:

$$
q_v
=
\text{vapour production or destruction}
$$

Therefore:

$$
q_v>0
\rightarrow
\text{vapour formation}
$$

$$
q_v<0
\rightarrow
\text{vapour collapse}
$$

---

## Rayleigh–Plesset equation

Bubble growth and collapse can be described using the Rayleigh–Plesset equation.

A simplified representation of the complete relation is:

$$
R\frac{d^2R}{dt^2}
+
\frac{3}{2}
\left(
\frac{dR}{dt}
\right)^2
=
\frac{p_s-p}{\rho_l}
-
\frac{2\sigma}{\rho_lR}
-
\frac{4\mu_l}{\rho_lR}
\frac{dR}{dt}
$$

where:

$R$ = bubble radius

$p_s$ = saturation pressure

$p$ = local liquid pressure

$\rho_l$ = liquid density

$\sigma$ = surface tension

$\mu_l$ = liquid viscosity

Main physical relation:

$$
p_s-p
\rightarrow
\text{bubble growth or collapse}
$$

If:

$$
p<p_s
$$

the pressure difference promotes bubble growth.

If:

$$
p>p_s
$$

the bubble tends to collapse.

---

## Simplified bubble-growth model

A simplified form relates bubble growth directly to the local pressure difference:

$$
\frac{dR}{dt}
=
\operatorname{sgn}(p_s-p)
\sqrt{
\frac{2|p_s-p|}{3\rho_l}
}
$$

Main advantage:

$$
\text{bubble growth rate}
=
f(\text{local pressure})
$$

The individual history of every bubble does not need to be explicitly tracked.

This relation can be used to build the source term $q_v$ in the vapour-fraction equation.

