# Main connection between Sections 13.3, 13.5 and 13.8

## Variable properties

$$
T,p
\rightarrow
\rho,\mu,\ldots
$$

## Free-surface flow

$$
\text{two fluids}
+
\text{moving interface}
$$

## Multiphase flow

$$
\text{multiple phases}
+
\text{possible momentum/mass/energy exchange}
$$

These topics are connected because multiphase and free-surface calculations often produce strong spatial variations of fluid properties.

For example:

$$
\text{air}
\rightarrow
\rho_{\text{air}}
$$

$$
\text{water}
\rightarrow
\rho_{\text{water}}
$$

Across the interface:

$$
\rho_{\text{air}}
\ll
\rho_{\text{water}}
$$

Therefore, the CFD solver must correctly handle:

$$
\boxed{
\text{phase distribution}
+
\text{variable properties}
+
\text{interface motion}
+
\text{coupling}
}
$$

---

# Main connection to atmospheric CFD

For the atmospheric boundary layer, the most relevant chain is:

$$
\text{surface}
\rightarrow
\text{friction}
\rightarrow
\text{wind shear}
\rightarrow
\text{turbulence}
$$

With thermal stratification:

$$
T
\rightarrow
\rho
\rightarrow
\text{buoyancy}
\rightarrow
\text{stability}
\rightarrow
\text{turbulence}
$$

At larger spatial and temporal scales:

$$
\text{Earth rotation}
\rightarrow
\text{Coriolis force}
\rightarrow
\text{wind field}
$$

For high-resolution atmospheric simulations:

$$
\text{3D turbulence}
\rightarrow
\text{LES}
$$

while larger-scale averaged simulations commonly rely more strongly on turbulence modelling such as RANS.

